import Foundation

/// Additional worked reasoning and equation-choice clues, spoken after the core lesson.
/// Original course lessons in Lessons.swift remain intact.
enum TeachingExtras {
    static let notes: [String: String] = [
        "math_variables": "A quick equation-selection tip. If the problem asks for acceleration and gives mass and the net force, use Newton's second law, sum of forces equals mass times acceleration. Isolate the unknown rather than guessing at a memorized rearrangement. As a spoken worked example, if net force is twenty newtons and mass is four kilograms, acceleration is twenty divided by four, or five meters per second squared.",
        "math_units": "Exam trap. Weight in pounds is a force, not automatically a mass. For example, a body weighing sixty-four point four pounds near Earth has a mass of two slugs because sixty-four point four divided by thirty-two point two is two. Check that the mass units match the inertia terms before you substitute into a dynamics equation.",
        "math_trig": "Decision procedure. First draw the force arrow. Second identify which axis the given angle is measured from. Third form a right triangle. The component touching the given angle uses cosine, and the opposite component uses sine. Finally give each component a plus or minus sign based on direction. Never memorize F x equals F cosine theta without checking how theta was drawn.",
        "math_radians": "Worked example. A wheel turns through one half revolution. Multiply one half by two pi and get pi radians. If the angular speed is three radians per second and the wheel radius is point two meters, the rim speed is radius times angular speed, or point six meters per second. Notice that radius must be in meters and angular speed in radians per second.",
        "math_derivative": "Spot the clue: if the problem gives position as a function of time and asks for velocity, take one time derivative. If it asks for acceleration, take two. For a simple example, if position is three t squared meters, velocity is six t meters per second and acceleration is six meters per second squared. Units tell you whether you took the right number of derivatives.",
        "math_chain": "Take a breath at each derivative. If x equals A cosine omega t, the first derivative is negative A omega sine omega t. The second derivative is negative A omega squared cosine omega t. That is negative omega squared times x. This is exactly the form needed in an undamped vibration equation.",
        "math_integral": "Choose the integration variable by the physical question. Force integrated over displacement gives work and changes kinetic energy. Force integrated over time gives impulse and changes linear momentum. Moment integrated over time gives angular impulse and changes angular momentum. If a torque starts at zero and increases linearly to ten newton meters over two seconds, the angular impulse is the triangular area, one half times two times ten, or ten newton meter seconds.",
        "math_quadratic": "For damped vibration, substitute the characteristic values a equals m, b equals c, and c in the usual quadratic formula equals spring stiffness k. To avoid confusing the two letter c's, say it as: negative damping coefficient plus or minus square root of damping coefficient squared minus four times mass times stiffness, all divided by two times mass. The sign under the square root determines the damping behavior.",
        "physics_newton": "Recognize the trigger words. If the question asks for force or instantaneous acceleration, start with Newton's law. If it describes a spring displaced from equilibrium, add the restoring force negative k times x. If a damper is attached, add negative c times velocity. Sum the forces to form m x double dot plus c x dot plus k x equals an applied force. This is the bridge into Chapter twenty-two.",
        "physics_moment": "Worked example. A twenty newton force acting with a perpendicular distance of point three meters creates a moment of six newton meters. If the problem gives an oblique force, use either the perpendicular lever arm or the force component perpendicular to the position vector. Do not multiply by the full slanted distance unless it really is perpendicular.",
        "physics_inertia": "For a uniform rod of length L and mass m, inertia about its center is one twelfth m L squared. About its end, use the parallel-axis theorem. Add mass times the square of half its length, which changes one twelfth plus one quarter into one third m L squared. Always identify the actual rotation axis before choosing an inertia formula.",
        "physics_energy": "Exam equation-choice test. Force over a known distance points toward work and energy. Force over a known time points toward impulse and momentum. Collision or impact points toward angular momentum about a clever pivot and perhaps restitution. A spring plus a position or amplitude points toward energy or a vibration equation. Start by circling what is given and what must be found.",
        "ch19_linear": "Start from linear momentum equals mass times center-of-mass velocity. If the center is fixed, the total linear momentum is zero. That does not mean the wheel cannot spin. If the center moves at two meters per second and mass is five kilograms, linear momentum has magnitude ten kilogram meters per second, directed with the center velocity.",
        "ch19_angular": "Equation selection. Angular momentum about the center G is I G times omega. About a fixed pivot O, use I O times omega. About a different point for a body with translating mass center, include the position cross momentum term. Then angular impulse equals the change in angular momentum. State the moment point before writing an equation.",
        "ch19_impulse": "Trigger words: torque applied for so many seconds, short time interval, or angular velocity before and after. Write initial angular momentum plus external angular impulse equals final angular momentum. For constant torque of three newton meters acting for four seconds, the angular impulse is twelve newton meter seconds. For time-varying torque, integrate rather than multiplying one sample value by four.",
        "ch19_conservation": "Collision procedure. First select the whole colliding system. Second choose a moment point, often the pin. Third ask whether any external angular impulse about that point matters during impact. The pin force has zero moment about the pin, even if its impulse is large. Fourth write angular momentum before equals angular momentum after when justified. Do not automatically conserve kinetic energy in an inelastic impact.",
        "ch22_shm": "Worked example. With mass four kilograms and stiffness one hundred newtons per meter, natural angular frequency is square root of one hundred divided by four, or five radians per second. Period is two pi over five seconds, about one point two six seconds. Ordinary frequency is five over two pi hertz. Angular frequency and hertz are different units, so label the final answer carefully.",
        "ch22_springs": "For two springs with stiffness four thousand and six thousand newtons per meter, parallel stiffness is ten thousand. In series, multiply four thousand times six thousand and divide by their sum, giving twenty-four hundred. Put the equivalent stiffness into the natural-frequency formula. If the drawing is confusing, ask: do both springs stretch the same distance, or do they carry the same force?",
        "ch22_ode": "Follow a fixed algorithm. One: write m x double dot plus c x dot plus k x equals zero. Two: assume an exponential e to the r t. Three: obtain m r squared plus c r plus k equals zero. Four: solve for r with the quadratic formula. Five: use the form of the roots to write the general response. Finally apply initial displacement and velocity to find the constants.",
        "ch22_forced": "Recognize forcing terms such as F zero cosine omega t. The general motion is a homogeneous or transient response plus a particular or forced response. If no damping is present and the driving frequency approaches the natural frequency, the ideal model predicts resonance. Carefully separate the driving frequency from natural frequency before using a formula.",
        "ch22_damping": "Damping classification shortcut. First compute critical damping c critical equals two square root of m k. Then form the damping ratio zeta equals c divided by c critical. Below one is underdamped and still oscillates. Equal to one is critically damped. Above one is overdamped and does not oscillate in the simple model. These labels describe the homogeneous response, not whether an external forcing term exists.",
        "ch22_forced_damped": "For damped forced vibration, separate the transient part, which decays, from the steady-state particular response, which remains while forcing continues. If the question asks what motion remains long after starting, think steady state. If it asks how the system responds immediately from its initial condition, include the transient as well. Always check whether the problem is asking for displacement amplitude, phase, or full time response."
    ]

    static func forLesson(_ lesson: Lesson) -> String {
        notes[lesson.id] ?? "Equation choice tip: identify the unknown, circle the given physical quantities, and explain why the chosen formula relates them."
    }
}
